4: Sum them up**\n\nFirst term: \\(21{,}160{,}000{,}000{,}000\\)\n\nSecond term: \\(303{,}406{,}800{,}000\\)\n\nSum: \\(21{,}463{,}406{,}800{,}000\\)\n\nAdd third term: \\(1{,}087{,}614{,}441\\)\n\nFinal sum:\n\n\\[\n21{,}464{,}494{,}414{,}441\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Final answer:**\n\nAnswer: 21464494414441  \nConfidence: 95%\n---\nAlright, let's compute \\( 4632979^2 \\) step by step.\n\nWe can write:\n\n\\[\n4632979 = 4{,}632{,}979\n\\]\n\nLet\u2019s use the identity \\((a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\\), but maybe easier is to treat as:\n\n\\[\n(4{,}600{,}000 + 32{,}979)^2\n\\]\n\n- \\( 4{,}600{,}000^2 = (46 \\times 10^5)^2 = 2116 \\times 10^{10} = 21{,}160{,}000{,}000{,}000 \\)\n- \\(2 \\times 4{,}600{,}000 \\times 32{,}979 = 9{,}200{,}000 \\times 32{,}979\\)\n\nCompute \\(32{,}979 \\times 9{,}200{,}000\\):\n\nFirst \\(32{,}979 \\times 92 = 3{,}034{,}068\\), then append four zeros: \\(30{,}340{,}680{,}000\\), but we must multiply by 100,000 because 9,200,000 = 92 \u00d7 100,000.\n\nWait: \\(9{,}200{,}000 = 92 \\times 100{,}000\\).\n\nSo \\(32{,}979 \\times 92 = 3{,}034{,}068\\), then \u00d7 100,000 = 303,406,800,000.\n\nSo \\(2ab = 303{,}406{,}800{,}000\\).\n\n- \\(b^2 = 32{,}979^2\\).\n\nCompute \\(32{,}979^2\\):\n\n\\((33{,}000 - 21)^2 = 33{,}000^2 - 2 \\times 33{,}000 \\times 21 + 21^2\\).\n\n\\(33{,}000^2 = 1{,}089{,}000{,}000\\).\n\n\\(2 \\times 33{,}000 \\times 21 = 66{,}000 \\times 21 = 1{,}386{,}000\\).\n\n\\(21^2 = 441\\).\n\nSo \\(32{,}979^2 = 1{,}089{,}000{,}000 - 1{,}386{,}000 + 441 = 1{,}087{,}614{,}441\\).\n\nNow sum:\n\n\\(21{,}160{,}000{,}000{,}000 + 303{,}406{,}800{,}000 = 21{,}463{,}406{,}800{,}000\\).\n\nAdd \\(1{,}087{,}614{,}441\\):\n\n\\(21{,}463{,}406{,}800{,}000 + 1{,}087{,}614{,}441 = 21{,}464{,}494{,}414{,}441\\).\n\nSo the square is:\n\n\\[\n21{,}464{,}494{,}414{,}441\n\\]\n\nLet\u2019s check quickly mod 9 or something:\n\n4632979 mod 9: sum digits = 4+6+3+2+9+7+9 = 40 \u2192 40 mod 9 = 4. Square mod 9 = 16 mod 9 = 7. Result's digits sum: 2+1+4+