 in groups of 3 digits:\n\n7339476609  \n\u2192 groups: 7 | 339 | 476 | 609.\n\nSo:\n\n\\( N = 7 \\times 1000^3 + 339 \\times 1000^2 + 476 \\times 1000 + 609 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Modulo 799, find powers of 1000.**\n\n\\( 1000 \\mod 799 = 201 \\).\n\nSo:\n\n- \\( 1000^1 \\equiv 201 \\)\n- \\( 1000^2 \\equiv 201^2 = 40401 \\). Now \\( 40401 \\mod 799 \\):\n\n799 \u00d7 50 = 39950, remainder 40401 \u2212 39950 = 451.\n\nSo \\( 1000^2 \\equiv 451 \\).\n\n- \\( 1000^3 \\equiv 451 \\times 201 = 90651 \\).\n\nNow mod 799: 799 \u00d7 113 = 90287, remainder 90651 \u2212 90287 = 364.\n\nSo \\( 1000^3 \\equiv 364 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Apply to each term.**\n\nTerm1: \\( 7 \\times 1000^3 \\equiv 7 \\times 364 = 2548 \\). Mod 799: 799 \u00d7 3 = 2397, remainder 151.\n\nTerm2: \\( 339 \\times 1000^2 \\equiv 339 \\times 451 = 152889 \\).  \nMod 799: 799 \u00d7 191 = 152609, remainder 280.\n\nTerm3: \\( 476 \\times 1000 \\equiv 476 \\times 201 = 95676 \\).  \n799 \u00d7 119 = 95081, remainder 595.\n\nTerm4: \\( 609 \\equiv 609 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 5: Sum remainders and reduce.**\n\n151 + 280 = 431.  \n431 + 595 = 1026.  \n1026 + 609 = 1635.\n\nNow \\( 1635 \\mod 799 \\): 799 \u00d7 2 = 1598, remainder 37.\n\n---\n\nSo the remainder is \\( 37 \\).\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 37  \nConfidence: 95%\n---\nLet's go step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder when \\( 7339476609 \\) is divided by \\( 799 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Recognize useful number theory tools.**  \nWe could do long division, but since 799 is close to 800, maybe compute modulo 799 directly with modular arithmetic.\n\n799 is prime? Let's check:  \n\\(\\sqrt{799} \\approx 28.26\\). Test primes up to 28:  \n799 mod 3 = 7+9+9=25, not divisible by 3.  \nmod 5 \u2192 no.  \nmod 7: 7\u00d7114=798, remainder 1 \u2192 not divisible.  \nmod 11: 11\u00d772=792, remainder 7 \u2192 no.  \nmod 13: 13\u00d761=793, remainder 6 \u2192 no.  \nmod 17: 17\u00d747=799? 17\u00d747=799 exactly. So 799=17\u00d747. Factorized: 799 = 17 \u00d7 47.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Chinese Remainder Theorem approach.**  \nWe can find remainder mod 17 and mod 47, then combine.\n\n**Mod 17:**  \nFirst reduce 7339476609 mod 17.\n\nLet\u2019s do stepwise mod 17:  \n7339476609 mod 17:  \nUse decimal chunk or direct:  \n\nBreak into chunks: Let\u2019s do mod 17 with the whole number via iterative method.  \nStart with remainder 0:  \nProcess digits \"7339476609\":  \n\n- 7 \u2192 r=7  \n- 7*10+3=73, 73 mod 17 = 73-68=5  \n- 5*10+3=53, 53 mod 17 = 53-51=2  \n- 2*10+9=29, 29 mod 17 = 12  \n- 12*10+4=124, 124 mod 17: 17\u00d77=119, remainder 5  \n- 5*10+7=57, 57 mod 17 = 57-51=6  \n- 6*10+6=66, 66 mod 17 = 66-51=15  \n- 15*10+6=156, 156 mod 17 = 156-153=3  \n- 3*10+0=30, 30 mod 17=13  \n- 13*10+9=139, 139 mod 17=139-136=3\n\nSo remainder mod 17 is 3.\n\n---\n\n**Mod 47:**  \nProcess digits again with mod 47:  \n\n- 7 \u2192 r=7  \n- 7*10+3=73, 73 mod 47 = 26  \n- 26*10+3=263, 47\u00d75=235, remainder 28  \n- 28*10+9=289, 47\u00d76=282, remainder 7  \n- 7*10+4=74, 74-47=27  \n- 27*10+7=277, 47\u00d75=235, remainder 42  \n- 42*10+6=426, 47\u00d79=423, remainder 3  \n- 3*10+6=36, remainder 36  \n- 36*10+0=360, 47\u00d77=329, remainder 31  \n- 31*10+9=319, 47\u00d76=282, remainder 37\n\nSo remainder mod 47 is 37.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Combine via CRT:**  \nWe need x \u2261 3 (mod 17), x \u2261 37 (mod 47).\n\nLet x = 3 + 17k.  \n3 + 17k \u2261 37 (mod 47) \u2192 17k \u2261 34 (mod 47).\n\nFind inverse of 17 mod 47:  \n47 = 2\u00d717 + 13  \n17 = 1\u00d713 + 4  \n13 = 3\u00d74 + 1 \u2192 back substitute:  \n1 = 13 - 3\u00d74  \n4 = 17 - 1\u00d713 \u2192 1 = 13 - 3\u00d7(17 - 13) = 4\u00d713 - 3\u00d717  \n13 = 47 - 2\u00d717 \u2192 1 = 4\u00d7(47 - 2\u00d717) - 3\u00d717 = 4\u00d747 - 8\u00d717 - 3\u00d717 = 4\u00d747 - 11\u00d717.\n\nThus -11\u00d717 \u2261 1 mod 47 \u2192 inverse of 17 is -11 \u2261 36 mod 47.\n\nMultiply: k \u2261 34\u00d736 mod 47.  \n34\u00d736=1224. 47\u00d726=1222, remainder 2. So k \u2261 2 mod 47.\n\nThus k=2 gives x=3+17\u00d72=37. That\u2019s less than 799, so remainder is 37? Wait check: 37 mod 17 is 3, mod 47 is 37. Yes.\n\nBut is the original number smaller than 799? No, it's huge, so the remainder should be 37? Let\u2019s check plausibility: 37 is less than 799, yes. But is that the actual remainder? Let\u2019s test small: 7339476609 mod 799 by direct division check if possible.\n\n---\n\n**Step 4 \u2013 Quick sanity check by direct mod 799:**  \nWe can do mod 800 then adjust:  \n7339476609 mod 800: last 3 digits 609? Wait mod 800 only needs last 3 digits? Actually 800=8\u00d7100, but easiest: mod 800 means last 3 digits? No, 800 divides 1000? 1000 mod 800=200, so not simple. Let\u2019s do mod 799 directly by chunks.\n\nUse: 10^3=1000 \u2261 201 mod 799.  \nBreak number into 3-digit chunks from right:  \n7339476609 \u2192 chunks: 7 | 339 | 476 | 609.\n\nCompute: start with 0:  \nval=7  \nval=(7\u00d71000+339) mod 799: 7\u00d71000=7000, 7000 mod 799: 799\u00d78=6392, remainder 608. +339=947, 947-799=148.  \nval=148  \nval=(148\u00d71000+476) mod 799: 148000 mod 799. 799\u00d7185=147815, remainder 185. +476=661.  \nval=661  \nval=(661\u00d71000+609) mod 799: 661000 mod 799: 799\u00d7827=660773, remainder 227. +609=836, 836-799=37.\n\nSo remainder = 37. Confirmed.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5 \u2013 Conclusion:**  \nThe remainder is indeed 37.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 37  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nLet\u2019s go step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder when \\( 7339476609 \\) is divided by \\( 799 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Modulo approach**  \nWe can compute \\( 7339476609 \\mod 799 \\) directly by long division or by breaking it down.\n\nFirst, check:  \n\\( 799 \\times 9,000,000 = 7,191,000,000 \\)  \nSubtract from 7,339,476,609:  \n\\( 7,339,476,609 - 7,191,000,000 = 148,476,609 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Continue dividing**  \n\\( 799 \\times 185,000 = 147,815,000 \\)  \nSubtract: \\( 148,476,609 - 147,815,000 = 661,609 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Continue**  \n\\( 799 \\times 800 = 639,200 \\)  \nSubtract: \\( 661,609 - 639,200 = 22,409 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 4 \u2013 Continue**  \n\\( 799 \\times 28 = 22,372 \\)  \nSubtract: \\( 22,409 - 22,372 = 37 \\).\n\n---\n\nSo the remainder is \\( 37 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Check:**  \n\\( 799 \\times (9,000,000 + 185,000 + 800 + 28) \\)  \n= \\( 799 \\times 9,185,828 = 7,339,476,572 \\)  \nAdd remainder 37 \u2192 \\( 7,339,476,609 \\). Correct.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 37  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nLet's compute step-by-step.\n\nWe want \\( 7339476609 \\bmod 799 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Break down the division.**\n\nFirst, note \\(799 = 800 - 1\\).\n\nWe can use the fact that \\(800 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{799}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Express the number in base 800 perhaps.**\n\nWrite \\(7339476609\\) in terms of powers of 800.\n\n- \\(800^3 = 512,000,000\\) (too large? Let's check: \\(800^3 = 512,000,000\\), yes, smaller than 7.3 billion. \\(800^4\\) would be huge.)\n- \\(800^2 = 640,000\\).\n\nDivide 7,339,476,609 by 800^3 = 512,000,000:\n\n\\(512,000,000 \\times 14 = 7,168,000,000\\).\n\nSubtract: \\(7,339,476,609 - 7,168,000,000 = 171,476,609\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Next term 800^2 = 640,000.**\n\n\\(171,476,609 / 640,000 = 267\\) remainder?\n\n\\(640,000 \\times 267 = 640,000 \\times 200 = 128,000,000\\), plus \\(640,000 \\times 67 = 42,880,000\\), sum = 170,880,000.\n\nSubtract: \\(171,476,609 - 170,880,000 = 596,609\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 4 \u2013 Next term 800^1 = 800.**\n\n\\(596,609 / 800 = 745\\) remainder?\n\n\\(800 \\times 745 = 596,000\\).\n\nSubtract: remainder \\(609\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 5 \u2013 Last term 800^0 = 1.**\n\n\\(609 / 1 = 609\\), remainder 0.\n\nSo base-800 representation: \\((14, 267, 745, 609)_{800}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 6 \u2013 Mod 799.**\n\nSince \\(800 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{799}\\), each power \\(800^k \\equiv 1^k = 1 \\pmod{799}\\).\n\nSo \\(7339476609 \\equiv 14 + 267 + 745 + 609 \\pmod{799}\\).\n\nSum: \\(14 + 267 = 281\\), plus 745 = 1026, plus 609 = 1635.\n\n---\n\n**Step 7 \u2013 Reduce mod 799.**\n\n\\(799 \\times 2 = 1598\\).\n\n\\(1635 - 1598 = 37\\).\n\nSo remainder = 37.\n\n---\n\n**Step 8 \u2013 Double-check quickly.**\n\nCheck with modular multiplication maybe? But the base-800 trick is solid since 800 \u2261 1 mod 799.\n\nYes, 37 seems correct.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 37  \nConfidence: 95%"
