    "raw": "Let's compute the remainder of \\( 8080460787 \\) divided by \\( 610 \\).\n\nFirst, note that \\( 610 = 61 \\times 10 \\).  \nWe can find the remainder modulo 610 by using the Chinese Remainder Theorem with mod 61 and mod 10.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Mod 10**  \nThe last digit is 7, so  \n\\( 8080460787 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{10} \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Mod 61**  \nWe need \\( 8080460787 \\bmod 61 \\).\n\nFirst, reduce 8080460787 modulo 61.\n\nWe can do this in steps:\n\n- \\( 61 \\times 132,466,570 = 8,080,460,770 \\) (let's check: \\( 61 \\times 132,466,570 = 61 \\times 132,000,000 = 8,052,000,000 \\), plus \\( 61 \\times 466,570 = 28,460,770 \\), sum = 8,080,460,770).  \nSubtract: \\( 8,080,460,787 - 8,080,460,770 = 17 \\).\n\nSo remainder mod 61 is 17.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Solve system**  \nWe have:  \n\\( x \\equiv 17 \\pmod{61} \\)  \n\\( x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{10} \\)\n\nLet \\( x = 17 + 61k \\).  \nMod 10: \\( 17 + 61k \\equiv 7 + k \\equiv 7 \\pmod{10} \\) (since 61 \u2261 1 mod 10, 17 \u2261 7 mod 10).  \nSo \\( 7 + k \\equiv 7 \\pmod{10} \\) \u2192 \\( k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{10} \\).\n\nSmallest nonnegative \\( k \\) is 0, so \\( x = 17 \\).\n\nCheck: 17 mod 61 = 17, 17 mod 10 = 7. Works.\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Check if remainder < 610**  \nYes, 17 < 610.\n\nSo the remainder is 17.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 17  \nConfidence: 95%"
