    "raw": "Probability: 95%\n---\nAlright, let's go step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder when \\( 8080460787 \\) is divided by \\( 610 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Break down the modulus**\n\n\\( 610 = 61 \\times 10 \\).\n\nWe can find the remainder modulo 61 and modulo 10, then combine.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Modulo 10**\n\nThe last digit of \\( 8080460787 \\) is 7, so:\n\n\\[\n8080460787 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Modulo 61**\n\nWe can reduce the number modulo 61.\n\nFirst, note \\( 61 \\times 132,000,000 = 8,052,000,000 \\), which is larger than our number, so let's do it more systematically.\n\nWe can use the fact that \\( 10^3 = 1000 \\equiv 1000 - 16\\times 61 = 1000 - 976 = 24 \\pmod{61} \\).\n\nBut maybe easier: break the number into chunks.\n\nNumber: \\( 8,080,460,787 \\).\n\nLet\u2019s do mod 61 step-by-step using base 1000 chunks:\n\nChunks: \\( 8 \\ | \\ 080 \\ | \\ 460 \\ | \\ 787 \\).\n\nThat is: \\( 8 \\times 1000^3 + 80 \\times 1000^2 + 460 \\times 1000 + 787 \\).\n\nWe know \\( 1000 \\equiv 24 \\pmod{61} \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 4 \u2013 Powers of 1000 mod 61**\n\n- \\( 1000 \\equiv 24 \\)\n- \\( 1000^2 \\equiv 24^2 = 576 \\). Now \\( 576 - 9\\times 61 = 576 - 549 = 27 \\). So \\( 1000^2 \\equiv 27 \\).\n- \\( 1000^3 \\equiv 27 \\times 24 = 648 \\). \\( 648 - 10\\times 61 = 648 - 610 = 38 \\).\n\nSo:\n\n\\( 1000^0 \\equiv 1 \\)  \n\\( 1000^1 \\equiv 24 \\)  \n\\( 1000^2 \\equiv 27 \\)  \n\\( 1000^3 \\equiv 38 \\)\n\n---\n\n**Step 5 \u2013 Compute mod 61**\n\nExpression: \\( 8\\times 38 + 80\\times 27 + 460\\times 24 + 787 \\).\n\nFirst term: \\( 8\\times 38 = 304 \\). \\( 304 - 5\\times 61 = 304 - 305 = -1 \\equiv 60 \\).\n\nSecond term: \\( 80\\times 27 = 2160 \\). \\( 61\\times 35 = 2135 \\), remainder \\( 2160 - 2135 = 25 \\).\n\nThird term: \\( 460\\times 24 = 11040 \\). \\( 61\\times 181 = 11041 \\), remainder \\( 11040 - 11041 = -1 \\equiv 60 \\).\n\nFourth term: \\( 787 \\). \\( 61\\times 12 = 732 \\), remainder \\( 55 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 6 \u2013 Sum remainders**\n\nSum: \\( 60 + 25 + 60 + 55 = 200 \\).\n\nNow \\( 200 \\mod 61 \\): \\( 61\\times 3 = 183 \\), remainder \\( 17 \\).\n\nSo:\n\n\\[\n8080460787 \\equiv 17 \\pmod{61}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 7 \u2013 Combine with mod 10**\n\nWe need \\( x \\equiv 17 \\pmod{61} \\) and \\( x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{10} \\).\n\nLet \\( x = 17 + 61k \\). Mod 10: \\( 17 + 61k \\equiv 7 + k \\equiv 7 \\pmod{10} \\).\n\nSo \\( k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{10} \\).\n\nSmallest nonnegative \\( k \\) is 0, so \\( x = 17 \\).\n\nCheck: 17 mod 61 is 17, mod 10 is 7. Works.\n\n---\n\nSince \\( 0 \\le 17 < 610 \\), the remainder is 17.\n\n---\n\n**Step 8 \u2013 Final answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{17}\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 17"
