    "raw": "Let's go step-by-step.\n\nWe want \\( 8080460787 \\mod 610 \\).\n\nFirst, note \\(610 = 61 \\times 10\\).  \nWe can find the remainder modulo 610 directly by division or by breaking into mod 61 and mod 10.  \nBut since 61 and 10 are coprime, we can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem approach.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Mod 10**  \nThe last digit is 7, so:  \n\\( 8080460787 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{10} \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Mod 61**  \nWe can do this using modular reduction.\n\nFirst, note \\(61 \\times 132,466,570 = ?\\) Let\u2019s do it more systematically.\n\nWe can use: \\( 10^3 = 1000 \\equiv 1000 - 16 \\times 61 = 1000 - 976 = 24 \\pmod{61} \\).  \nBut maybe easier: Break number into chunks.\n\nAlternatively, just compute directly:  \n\nFirst, \\( 8080460787 \\mod 61 \\).\n\nLet\u2019s reduce stepwise:\n\n- \\( 61 \\times 132,466,570 = 61 \\times 132,000,000 + 61 \\times 466,570 \\) \u2014 actually let's do it simpler:  \n\nWe can use: \\( 10^2 = 100 \\equiv 39 \\pmod{61} \\) (since 100 - 61 = 39).  \n\\( 10^3 = 390 \\equiv 390 - 6\\times 61 = 390 - 366 = 24 \\pmod{61} \\).  \n\\( 10^4 = 240 \\equiv 240 - 3\\times 61 = 240 - 183 = 57 \\equiv -4 \\pmod{61} \\).  \n\\( 10^5 = -40 \\equiv 21 \\pmod{61} \\).  \n\\( 10^6 = 210 \\equiv 210 - 3\\times 61 = 210 - 183 = 27 \\pmod{61} \\).  \n\\( 10^7 = 270 \\equiv 270 - 4\\times 61 = 270 - 244 = 26 \\pmod{61} \\).  \n\\( 10^8 = 260 \\equiv 260 - 4\\times 61 = 260 - 244 = 16 \\pmod{61} \\).  \n\\( 10^9 = 160 \\equiv 160 - 2\\times 61 = 160 - 122 = 38 \\pmod{61} \\).\n\nNow the number: \\(8080460787\\) has digits: 8,0,8,0,4,6,0,7,8,7 (10 digits). Let's index from right (position 0 for units):\n\n- pos0: 7 \u00d7 1 = 7\n- pos1: 8 \u00d7 10 \u2261 8\u00d710=80 \u2261 80-61=19\n- pos2: 7 \u00d7 10^2 \u2261 7\u00d739=273 \u2261 273-4\u00d761=273-244=29\n- pos3: 0 \u00d7 10^3 = 0\n- pos4: 6 \u00d7 10^4 \u2261 6\u00d757=342 \u2261 342-5\u00d761=342-305=37\n- pos5: 4 \u00d7 10^5 \u2261 4\u00d721=84 \u2261 84-61=23\n- pos6: 0 \u00