, too big. So remainder really is 335. So CRT says 457, but actual says 335. Contradiction \u2192 so one of mod 11 or mod 61 values is wrong.\n\nLet's check 335 mod 11: 11\u00d730=330, remainder 5. But we need remainder 6 mod 11. So 335 fails mod 11 check. So indeed 335 is not correct? Wait, that means direct division was wrong? Let's check multiplication again:\n\nActually 671\u00d7101,760:  \n671\u00d7100,000 = 67,100,000.  \n671\u00d71,760: 671\u00d71,000=671,000; \u00d7700=469,700; \u00d760=40,260. Sum for 1,760: 671,000+469,700=1,140,700; +40,260=1,180,960. Sum total=68,280,960. Difference=335. Yes.\n\nBut 335 mod 11 = 5, not 6. So perhaps original number mod 11 is not 6? Let's recompute mod 11 carefully:\n\nNumber: 68,281,295.  \nMod 11: Use 10 \u2261 -1:  \nWe can reduce: 68,281,295 mod 11:  \n11\u00d76,207,390=68,281,290, remainder 5. Yes! So remainder is 5, not 6. Earlier alternating sum was wrong. Let's check: digits 6 8 2 8 1 2 9 5. Alternating sum from left: 6-8=-2; +2=0; -8=-8; +1=-7; -2=-9; +9=0; -5=-5. But -5 \u2261 6 mod 11, but actual is 5. So discrepancy \u2014 maybe I did alternating sum wrong because of even/odd length? Actually for mod 11, the rule: sum of digits in odd positions (from right) minus sum in even positions (from right). Let's do from right:\n\nPositions from right (1-based):  \npos1:5, pos2:9, pos3:2, pos4:1, pos5:8, pos6:2, pos7:8, pos8:6.\n\nSum odd positions: pos1+pos3+pos5+pos7 = 5+2+8+8=23.  \nSum even positions: pos2+pos4+pos6+pos8 = 9+1+2+6=18.  \nDifference=23-18=5. Yes remainder 5 mod 11. So earlier error: I used left-to-right alternating sum starting plus, but since"
