act that \\( 10^6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\) (since \\( 10^6 = 1,000,000 \\) and \\( 1,000,000 \\bmod 7 = 1 \\) because \\( 999,999 \\) is divisible by 7).\n\nWrite \\( 9698924284 \\) in groups of 6 digits from the right:\n\n- Group 1 (rightmost 6 digits): \\( 924284 \\)\n- Group 2 (remaining left): \\( 9698 \\)\n\nCheck: \\( 9698 \\times 10^6 + 924284 \\).\n\nSince \\( 10^6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\),  \n\\( 9698924284 \\equiv 9698 + 924284 \\pmod{7} \\).\n\nSum: \\( 9698 + 924284 = 933982 \\).\n\nNow reduce 933982 mod 7:\n\n- \\( 7 \\times 133426 = 933982 \\) exactly? Let's check: \\( 133426 \\times 7 = 933982 \\) yes.  \nSo remainder is 0 mod 7.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Modulo 47**\n\nWe need \\( 9698924284 \\bmod 47 \\).\n\nWe can use \\( 10^3 = 1000 \\). \\( 1000 \\bmod 47 \\): \\( 47 \\times 21 = 987 \\), remainder 13. So \\( 10^3 \\equiv 13 \\pmod{47} \\).\n\nBut perhaps easier: break into chunks base 1000.\n\nNumber: \\( 9,698,924,284 \\) \u2192 groups of 3 digits from right:\n\n- Group 0: 284\n- Group 1: 924\n- Group 2: 698\n- Group 3: 9\n\nSo: \\( 9 \\times 1000^3 + 698 \\times 1000^2 + 924 \\times 1000 + 284 \\).\n\nFirst compute powers of 1000 mod 47:\n\n- \\( 1000 \\equiv 13 \\pmod{47} \\)\n- \\( 1000^2 \\equiv 13^2 = 169 \\). \\( 169 - 47 \\times 3 = 169 - 141 = 28 \\). So \u2261 28.\n- \\( 1000^3 \\equiv 28 \\times 13 = 364 \\). \\( 364 - 47 \\times 7 = 364 - 329 = 35 \\). So \u2261 35.\n\nNow compute:\n\nTerm 1: \\( 9 \\times 35 = 315 \\). \\( 315 - 47 \\times 6 = 315 - 282 = 33 \\).\n\nTerm 2: \\( 698 \\times 28 \\). First reduce 698 mod 47: \\( 47 \\times 14 = 658 \\), remainder 40. So \\( 40 \\times 28 = 1120 \\). Now 1120 mod 47: \\( 47 \\times 23 = 1081 \\), remainder 39.\n\nTerm 3: \\( 924 \\times 13 \\). Reduce 924 mod 47: \\( 47 \\times 19 = 893 \\), remainder 31. Then \\( 31 \\times 13 = 403 \\). \\( 403 - 47 \\times 8 = 403 - 376 = 27 \\).\n\nTerm 4: 284 mod 47: \\( 47 \\times 6 = 282 \\), remainder 2.\n\nSum these remainders: \\( 33 + 39 + 27 + 2 = 1