13 Mod 13**  \nUse \\(10^3 = 1000 \\equiv 1000 - 13 \\times 76 = 1000 - 988 = 12 \\equiv -1\\) mod 13.  \nSo \\(10^3 \\equiv -1\\) mod 13.\n\nGroups again: 6 | 252 | 434 | 352.\n\nEach group mod 13:  \n- 352 mod 13: \\(13 \\times 27 = 351\\) \u2192 remainder 1.  \n- 434 mod 13: \\(13 \\times 33 = 429\\) \u2192 remainder 5.  \n- 252 mod 13: \\(13 \\times 19 = 247\\) \u2192 remainder 5.  \n- 6 mod 13 = 6.\n\nPowers of 1000 mod 13:  \nFrom rightmost group: \\( (1000)^0 = 1\\), next \\( (1000)^1 \\equiv -1\\), next \\( (1000)^2 \\equiv 1\\), next \\( (1000)^3 \\equiv -1\\).\n\nSo:  \nRightmost group (352) \u00d7 1 \u2192 1  \nNext (434) \u00d7 (-1) \u2192 -5 \u2261 8 mod 13  \nNext (252) \u00d7 1 \u2192 5  \nLeftmost (6) \u00d7 (-1) \u2192 -6 \u2261 7 mod 13.\n\nSum: \\(1 + 8 + 5 + 7 = 21\\) mod 13 \u2192 \\(21 - 13 = 8\\).\n\nSo remainder mod 13 = 8.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5 \u2013 Combine via CRT**  \nWe have:  \n- mod 2: 0  \n- mod 27: 18  \n- mod 13: 8.\n\nFirst combine mod 27 and mod 13 (they're coprime):\n\nWe want \\(x \\equiv 18 \\pmod{27}\\), \\(x \\equiv 8 \\pmod{13}\\).\n\nLet \\(x = 18 + 27k\\).  \nMod 13: \\(18 \\equiv 5\\), \\(27 \\equiv 1\\) mod 13.  \nSo \\(5 + k \\equiv 8\\) mod 13 \u2192 \\(k \\equiv 3\\) mod 13.\n\nSmallest k = 3 \u2192 \\(x = 18 + 81 = 99\\).\n\nCheck: 99 mod 27 = 18, 99 mod 13 = 99 - 91 = 8. Good.\n\nNow mod 351 (27\u00d713), x \u2261 99.\n\n---\n\n**Step 6 \u2013 Incorporate mod 2**  \nWe need x \u2261 99 mod 351 and x even (mod 2 = 0).  \n351 is odd, so adding multiples of 351 flips parity.\n\n99 is odd, we need even \u2192 add 351 once: 99 + 351 = 450 (even). Check mod 351 = 99.\n\nSo x \u2261 450 mod 702.\n\n---\n\n**Step 7 \u2013 Check**  \n450 mod 2 = 0, mod 27: 450 - 27\u00d716=450-432=18, mod 13: 450-13\u00d734=450-442=8. All match.\n\nThus remainder = 450.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 450  \nConfidence: 95%\n---\nAlright, let's go step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder when \\( 6252434352 \\) is divided by \\( 702 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Prime factorization of 702**\n\n\\( 702 = 2 \\times 351 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 117 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 39 = 2 \\times 3^3 \\times 13 \\).\n\nSo \\( 702 = 2 \\times 27 \\times 13 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Use Chinese Remainder Theorem approach**\n\nWe can find the remainder mod 702 by finding remainders mod 2, mod 27, and mod 13, then combine.\n\n---\n\n**Mod 2:**\n6252434352 is even \u2192 remainder 0 mod 2.\n\n---\n\n**Mod 27:**\nFirst, \\( 10^3 = 1000 \\equiv 1 \\mod 27 \\) (since 1000 \u2212 27\u00d737 = 1000 \u2212 999 = 1).\n\nSo group digits in blocks of 3 from the right:\n\nNumber: 6,252,434,352 \u2192 groups: \\( 6 \\,|\\, 252 \\,|\\, 434 \\,|\\, 352 \\).\n\nCheck last block is 352, yes.\n\nWe reduce each block mod 27:\n\n- 6 mod 27 = 6  \n- 252 mod 27: 27\u00d79=243, remainder 9  \n- 434 mod 27: 27\u00d716=432, remainder 2  \n- 352 mod 27: 27\u00d713=351, remainder 1  \n\nBecause \\( 1000 \\equiv 1 \\mod 27 \\), each block is multiplied by \\( 1000^k \\equiv 1 \\mod 27 \\).\n\nSo total mod 27 = (6 + 9 + 2 + 1) mod 27 = 18 mod 27.\n\nSo remainder mod 27 is 18.\n\n---\n\n**Mod 13:**\n\\( 10^3 = 1000 \\mod 13\\): 13\u00d776=988, remainder 12, i.e., \u22121 mod 13.\n\nSo 1000 \u2261 \u22121 mod 13.\n\nAgain groups: 6 | 252 | 434 | 352.\n\nFrom right (least significant group first):\n\nBlock0 = 352 mod 13: 13\u00d727=351 \u2192 remainder 1. Multiplied by (1000^0 \u2261 1) \u2192 1.\n\nBlock1 = 434 mod 13: 13\u00d733=429, remainder 5. Multiplied by (1000^1 \u2261 \u22121) \u2192 \u22125 \u2261 8 mod 13.\n\nBlock2 = 252 mod 13: 13\u00d719=247, remainder 5. Multiplied by (1000^2 \u2261 1) \u2192 5.\n\nBlock3 = 6 mod 13 = 6. Multiplied by (1000^3 \u2261 \u22121) \u2192 \u22126 \u2261 7 mod 13.\n\nSum: 1 + 8 + 5 + 7 = 21 \u2261 8 mod 13.\n\nSo remainder mod 13 is 8.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Solve system**\n\nWe have:\n\n\\( x \\equiv 0 \\mod 2 \\)  \n\\( x \\equiv 18 \\mod 27 \\)  \n\\( x \\equiv 8 \\mod 13 \\)\n\nFrom mod 2 and mod 27: since gcd(2,27)=1, we can find mod 54.\n\nCheck 18 mod 2 = 0, so 18 works for both. So x \u2261 18 mod 54.\n\nNow solve:\n\nx \u2261 18 mod 54  \nx \u2261 8 mod 13.\n\nLet x = 18 + 54k.\n\n18 + 54k \u2261 8 mod 13.\n\n18 mod 13 = 5, 54 mod 13 = 2 (since 52 is multiple of 13). So:\n\n5 + 2k \u2261 8 mod 13 \u2192 2k \u2261 3 mod 13.\n\nInverse of 2 mod 13 is 7 (since 2\u00d77=14\u22611). Multiply: k \u2261 21 \u2261 8 mod 13.\n\nSo k = 8 + 13t.\n\nx = 18 + 54\u00d78 + 54\u00d713t = 18 + 432 + 702t = 450 + 702t.\n\nThus remainder is 450.\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Verification**\n\nCheck 450 mod 2 = 0 \u2705  \n450 mod 27: 27\u00d716=432, remainder 18 \u2705  \n450 mod 13: 13\u00d734=442, remainder 8 \u2705\n\nSo remainder is indeed 450.\n\n---\n\n**Final answer:**\n\nAnswer: 450  \nConfidence: 95%\n---\nAlright, let's go step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder when \\( 6252434352 \\) is divided by \\( 702 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Factor the divisor**  \n\\( 702 = 2 \\times 351 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 117 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 39 = 2 \\times 3^3 \\times 13 \\).  \nSo \\( 702 = 2 \\times 27 \\times 13 \\).\n\nActually:  \n\\( 702 = 2 \\times 3^3 \\times 13 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Use modular arithmetic**  \nWe could directly do long division, but let's find the remainder mod 702 using the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) with mod 2, mod 27, and mod 13.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Mod 2**  \n6252434352 is even (ends in 2), so remainder mod 2 = 0.\n\n---\n\n**Step 4 \u2013 Mod 27**  \nFirst note \\( 10^3 = 1000 \\equiv 1 \\mod 27 \\) (since \\( 27 \\times 37 = 999\\)).  \nSo group digits in threes from the right:\n\nNumber: 6,252,434,352  \nGroups: 6 | 252 | 434 | 352\n\nCheck: \\( 6 \\times 10^9 + 252 \\times 10^6 + 434 \\times 10^3 + 352 \\)  \nMod 27: \\( 10^3 \\equiv 1\\), so \\( 10^6 \\equiv 1\\), \\(10^9 \\equiv 1\\).\n\nThus mod 27: sum groups = \\(6 + 252 + 434 + 352\\).\n\nSum: 6+252=258, +434=692, +352=1044.  \n1044 mod 27: 27\u00d738=1026, remainder 18.  \nSo remainder mod 27 = 18.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5 \u2013 Mod 13**  \nUse 10^3 = 1000 \u2261 1000 - 13\u00d776=1000-988=12 \u2261 -1 mod 13.  \nWait, 13\u00d776=988, remainder 12, so indeed 10^3 \u2261 -1 mod 13.\n\nThat means 10^6 \u2261 1 mod 13, 10^9 \u2261 -1 mod 13.\n\nGroups again: 6 | 252 | 434 | 352 with powers 10^9, 10^6, 10^3, 10^0.\n\nMod 13:  \n10^9 \u2261 -1 \u2192 term1 = 6\u00d7(-1) = -6  \n10^6 \u2261 1 \u2192 term2 = 252\u00d71 = 252  \n10^3 \u2261 -1 \u2192 term3 = 434\u00d7(-1) = -434  \n10^0 = 1 \u2192 term4 = 352.\n\nSum = -6 + 252 - 434 + 352.\n\n-6+252=246, 246-434=-188, -188+352=164.\n\n164 mod 13: 13\u00d712=156, remainder 8.  \nSo remainder mod 13 = 8.\n\n---\n\n**Step 6 \u2013 Solve CRT**  \nWe have:  \nr \u2261 0 mod 2  \nr \u2261 18 mod 27  \nr \u2261 8 mod 13\n\nFirst combine mod 2 and mod 27: since 18 is even, it satisfies mod 2 requirement (18 mod 2=0). So r \u2261 18 mod 54 (since lcm(2,27)=54).\n\nCheck 18 mod 2=0, yes.\n\nSo now: r \u2261 18 mod 54, r \u2261 8 mod 13.\n\nLet r = 18 + 54k.  \nMod 13: 18 \u2261 5, 54 \u2261 2 (since 52 is multiple of 13). So:  \n5 + 2k \u2261 8 mod 13 \u2192 2k \u2261 3 mod 13.\n\nInverse of 2 mod 13 is 7 (since 2\u00d77=14\u22611). Multiply: k \u2261 21 \u2261 8 mod 13.\n\nSmallest k=8 \u2192 r = 18 + 54\u00d78 = 18 + 432 = 450.\n\nCheck: 450 < 702, good.  \nCheck mod 27: 450-27\u00d716=450-432=18, yes.  \nCheck mod 13: 450-13\u00d734=450-442=8, yes.  \nCheck mod 2: 450 even, yes.\n\n---\n\n**Step 7 \u2013 Conclusion**  \nThe remainder is 450.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 450  \nConfidence: 95%\n---\nLet's compute the remainder of \\( 6252434352 \\) divided by \\( 702 \\).\n\nFirst, note that \\( 702 = 2 \\times 351 = 2 \\times 3^3 \\times 13 \\).\n\nWe can do the division step-by-step.\n\nLet\u2019s compute \\( 6252434352 \\mod 702 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Break into manageable parts.**\n\nWe can use modulus properties. Let\u2019s do it with direct long division approach or modular arithmetic.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Reduce modulo 702.**\n\nWe can first reduce modulo 2:  \nThe number is even, so remainder mod 2 is 0.\n\nNow reduce modulo 351 (since 702 = 2 \u00d7 351, and we want mod 702 \u2014 better to do mod 702 directly).\n\n---\n\nActually, let's do direct mod:\n\nWe can note \\( 702 \\times 8,000,000 = 5,616,000,000 \\).  \nSubtract:  \n\\( 6,252,434,352 - 5,616,000,000 = 636,434,352 \\).\n\nNow \\( 702 \\times 900,000 = 631,800,000 \\).  \nSubtract: \\( 636,434,352 - 631,800,000 = 4,634,352 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Continue.**  \n\\( 702 \\times 6,000 = 4,212,000 \\).  \nSubtract: \\( 4,634,352 - 4,212,000 = 422,352 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 4:**  \n\\( 702 \\times 600 = 421,200 \\).  \nSubtract: \\( 422,352 - 421,200 = 1,152 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 5:**  \n\\( 702 \\times 1 = 702 \\).  \nSubtract: \\( 1,152 - 702 = 450 \\).\n\n450 is less than 702, so remainder is 450.\n\n---\n\nLet\u2019s double-check quickly mod 2: 450 is even (0 mod 2), good. Mod 3: sum digits of 450 = 9 divisible by 3, good. Mod 13: 450 mod 13: 13\u00d734=442, remainder 8. Let\u2019s check original mod 13 quickly:  \nWe can check 6252434352 mod 13:  \n13\u00d7481,000,000 = 6,253,000,000 (too big?), let's do smaller:\n\nActually quicker: 6252434352 mod 13:  \n13\u00d7481,000,000 = 6,253,000,000. Difference: 6252434352 - 6253000000 = -565648 (negative), so skip.\n\nBetter: Use mod directly: 10^3 \u2261 -? maybe too long. Trust the division? Let\u2019s check 450 mod 13 = 8; need original mod 13 = 8.\n\nCheck original mod 13:  \n13\u00d7481,000,000 = 6,253,000,000 \u2192 remainder -565,648.  \n-565,648 mod 13: 13\u00d743,511 = 565,643, remainder -5 \u2192 8 mod 13. Yes, matches. Good.\n\nSo remainder is indeed 450.\n\n---\n\n**Final check:** 702\u00d78,906,000 = 6,252,012,000? Let's see: 8,906,000\u00d7702 = 8,906,000\u00d7700 + 8,906,000\u00d72 = 6,234,200,000 + 17,812,000 = 6,252,012,000. Difference from original: 6,252,434,352 - 6,252,012,000 = 422,352, matches earlier. 422,352 - 421,200=1,152, minus 702 = 450. Correct.\n\n---