    "raw": "Probability: 95%\n---\nAlright, let's go step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder when \\( 23617158 \\) is divided by \\( 900 \\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 1 \u2013 Break down 900\n\\( 900 = 9 \\times 100 \\), and since \\( \\gcd(9,100) = 1 \\), we can find the remainder modulo 9 and modulo 100 separately, then combine.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2 \u2013 Modulo 100\nThe last two digits of \\( 23617158 \\) are \\( 58 \\).  \nSo:\n\n\\[\n23617158 \\equiv 58 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 \u2013 Modulo 9\nSum of digits of \\( 23617158 \\):\n\n\\( 2+3+6+1+7+1+5+8 = 33 \\)  \n\\( 33 \\bmod 9 = 6 \\) (since \\( 27 \\) is divisible by 9, remainder 6).\n\nSo:\n\n\\[\n23617158 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 4 \u2013 Combine using Chinese Remainder Theorem\nWe need a number \\( x \\) such that:\n\n\\[\nx \\equiv 58 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\equiv 6 \\pmod{9}\n\\]\n\nLet \\( x = 58 + 100k \\).  \nModulo 9: \\( 58 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{9} \\) (since \\( 54 \\) is multiple of 9, remainder 4).  \n\\( 100 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9} \\).\n\nSo:\n\n\\[\n4 + 1\\cdot k \\equiv 6 \\pmod{9}\n\\]\n\\[\nk \\equiv 2 \\pmod{9}\n\\]\n\nSmallest \\( k = 2 \\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 \u2013 Find \\( x \\)\n\\( x = 58 + 100 \\times 2 = 258 \\).\n\nCheck: \\( 258 < 900 \\), so remainder is 258.\n\n---\n\n**Final check**:  \n- Mod 100: 58 \u2713  \n- Mod 9: \\( 2+5+8=15 \\), \\( 15 \\bmod 9 = 6 \\) \u2713\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 258"
