    "raw": "Let's compute \\( 23617158 \\mod 900 \\).\n\nFirst, note that \\( 900 = 9 \\times 100 \\), but since 9 and 100 are not coprime? Actually, they are coprime: \\( \\gcd(9,100)=1 \\). We can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n\n**Step 1: Mod 100**\n\nThe last two digits of 23617158 are 58, so:\n\n\\[\n23617158 \\equiv 58 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Mod 9**\n\nSum of digits of 23617158:\n\n\\( 2+3+6+1+7+1+5+8 = 33 \\).  \nThen \\( 33 \\mod 9 = 6 \\).\n\nSo:\n\n\\[\n23617158 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{9}\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Solve system**\n\nWe want \\( x \\equiv 58 \\pmod{100} \\) and \\( x \\equiv 6 \\pmod{9} \\).\n\nLet \\( x = 58 + 100k \\).\n\nMod 9: \\( 58 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{9} \\) (since 54 is multiple of 9, 58-54=4).  \n\\( 100 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9} \\).\n\nSo \\( 4 + k \\equiv 6 \\pmod{9} \\) \u2192 \\( k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{9} \\).\n\nSmallest \\( k = 2 \\):\n\n\\( x = 58 + 200 = 258 \\).\n\nCheck: \\( 258 < 900 \\), correct.  \nCheck mod 9: \\( 2+5+8=15 \\), \\( 15 \\mod 9 = 6 \\), correct.  \nMod 100: 58, correct.\n\nThus the remainder is \\( 258 \\).\n\n**Final check**:  \n\\( 23617158 - 258 = 23616900 \\). Divide by 900: \\( 23616900/900 = 26241 \\), integer. Good.\n\nAnswer: 258  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nFirst, note that 900 = 9 \u00d7 100.\n\n**Step 1: Remainder modulo 9**  \nTo find 23617158 mod 9, sum the digits:  \n2 + 3 + 6 + 1 + 7 + 1 + 5 + 8 = 33.  \n33 mod 9 = 6 (since 27 is divisible by 9, remainder 6).  \nSo 23617158 \u2261 6 (mod 9).\n\n**Step 2: Remainder modulo 100**  \nThe last two digits are 58, so the number \u2261 58 (mod 100).\n\n**Step 3: Solve using Chinese Remainder Theorem**  \nWe want a number N such that:  \nN \u2261 6 (mod 9)  \nN \u2261 58 (mod 100)  \n\nFrom N \u2261 58 (mod 100), possible N are 58, 158, 258, 358, 458, ..., up to less than 900.  \nCheck mod 9:  \n58 mod 9 = 4 (since 54 divisible, remainder 4) \u2014 no.  \n158 mod 9: 1+5+8=14 \u2192 14 mod 9 = 5 \u2014 no.  \n258: 2+5+8=15 \u2192 6 mod 9 \u2014 yes, works.\n\n**Step 4: Verify**  \n258 mod 9 = 6, mod 100 = 58. So remainder is 258.\n\nAnswer: 258  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nLet's compute the remainder of 23617158 divided by 900.\n\nFirst, note that 900 = 9 \u00d7 100.\n\n**Step 1: Mod 100**  \nThe last two digits of 23617158 are 58.  \nSo, 23617158 \u2261 58 (mod 100).\n\n**Step 2: Mod 9**  \nSum of digits of 23617158:  \n2 + 3 + 6 + 1 + 7 + 1 + 5 + 8 = 33.  \n33 mod 9 = 6.  \nSo, 23617158 \u2261 6 (mod 9).\n\n**Step 3: Combine via Chinese Remainder Theorem**  \nWe want a number r (0 \u2264 r < 900) such that:  \nr \u2261 58 (mod 100)  \nr \u2261 6 (mod 9)\n\nFrom r \u2261 58 (mod 100), possible r = 58 + 100k.\n\nCheck mod 9:  \n58 mod 9 = 4 (since 9\u00d76=54, remainder 4).  \n100 mod 9 = 1.  \nSo 58 + 100k \u2261 4 + k (mod 9).  \nWe need 4 + k \u2261 6 (mod 9) \u2192 k \u2261 2 (mod 9).\n\nSmallest k = 2 gives r = 58 + 200 = 258.\n\nCheck: 258 mod 100 = 58, 258 mod 9 = 6 (since 9\u00d728=252, remainder 6). Works.\n\nSo remainder is 258.\n\n**Final check** by direct division: 900 \u00d7 26241 = 23616900, which is more than 23617158? Wait, 900\u00d726241 = 23,616,900, which is greater than 23,617,158?  \nLet's check: 23,617,158 - 23,616,900 = 258. Yes, so 23,617,158 = 900\u00d726241 + 258. Remainder 258.\n\nAnswer: 258  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nFirst, note that \\(900 = 9 \\times 100\\). Since \\(100\\) and \\(9\\) are coprime, we can find the remainder modulo 9 and modulo 100, then combine.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Modulo 100**\n\nWe only need the last two digits of \\(23617158\\), which are \\(58\\).  \nSo \\(23617158 \\equiv 58 \\pmod{100}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Modulo 9**\n\nSum of digits of \\(23617158\\):  \n\\(2+3+6+1+7+1+5+8 = 33\\).  \n\\(33 \\bmod 9 = 6\\).  \nSo \\(23617158 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{9}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Solve system**\n\nWe need \\(x \\equiv 58 \\pmod{100}\\) and \\(x \\equiv 6 \\pmod{9}\\).\n\nFrom \\(x \\equiv 58 \\pmod{100}\\), \\(x = 58 + 100k\\).\n\nModulo 9:  \n\\(58 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{9}\\) (since \\(54\\) is multiple of 9).  \n\\(100 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9}\\).  \nSo \\(4 + 1k \\equiv 6 \\pmod{9}\\) \u2192 \\(k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{9}\\).\n\nThus \\(k = 2 + 9t\\).\n\nThen \\(x = 58 + 100(2 + 9t) = 58 + 200 + 900t = 258 + 900t\\).\n\nThe smallest nonnegative remainder is \\(258\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Check**\n\n\\(258 \\bmod 100 = 58\\) \u2705  \n\\(258 \\bmod 9\\): \\(2+5+8=15\\), \\(15 \\bmod 9 = 6\\) \u2705  \n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nAnswer: 258  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nFirst, let's find the remainder when 23617158 is divided by 900.\n\nWe can break 900 into \\(9 \\times 100\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Modulo 100:**\nThe last two digits of 23617158 are 58.  \nSo \\(23617158 \\mod 100 = 58\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Modulo 9:**\nSum of digits of 23617158:  \n2 + 3 + 6 + 1 + 7 + 1 + 5 + 8 = 33.  \n33 mod 9 = 6.  \nSo \\(23617158 \\mod 9 = 6\\).\n\n---\n\nWe need a number \\(x\\) such that:\n\n- \\(x \\equiv 58 \\pmod{100}\\)\n- \\(x \\equiv 6 \\pmod{9}\\)\n\nSince 100 \u2261 1 mod 9, let \\(x = 58 + 100k\\).  \nMod 9: 58 \u2261 4 (since 54 is multiple of 9, remainder 4), 100 \u2261 1 (mod 9).  \nSo \\(4 + k \\equiv 6 \\pmod{9}\\) \u21d2 \\(k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{9}\\).\n\nSmallest nonnegative k = 2 \u21d2 \\(x = 58 + 200 = 258\\).\n\nCheck: 258 mod 100 = 58, 258 mod 9 = 6 (since 252 is multiple of 9, remainder 6). Good.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Ensure it\u2019s < 900:**  \n258 is less than 900, so remainder is 258.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 258  \nConfidence: 100%"
