    "raw": "Let\u2019s go step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder when \\( 58793812 \\) is divided by \\( 572 \\).\n\nFirst, note that \\(572 = 4 \\times 143 = 4 \\times 11 \\times 13\\).\n\nWe can reduce the problem using modular arithmetic.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Check divisibility by 4**  \nThe last two digits are 12, which is divisible by 4, so \\(58793812 \\bmod 4 = 0\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Modulo 143**  \nWe use \\( 143 = 11 \\times 13 \\).\n\nFirst, modulo 11:  \nAlternating sum of digits:  \nDigits: 5,8,7,9,3,8,1,2.  \nFrom right to left: 2 - 1 + 8 - 3 + 9 - 7 + 8 - 5 =  \n(2 - 1) = 1, +8 = 9, -3 = 6, +9 = 15, -7 = 8, +8 = 16, -5 = 11.  \n11 mod 11 = 0. So divisible by 11.\n\nModulo 13:  \nLet\u2019s compute \\(58793812 \\bmod 13\\).  \nWe can do this step-by-step:\n\n- 13 \u00d7 4,522,600 = 58,793,800, remainder 12. Wait, let\u2019s check:  \n13 \u00d7 4,522,600: 4,522,600 \u00d7 10 = 45,226,000; \u00d73 = 13,567,800; sum = 58,793,800. Yes, remainder 12.\n\nSo 58793812 \u2261 12 mod 13.\n\n---\n\nWe have:\n\n- mod 11: 0  \n- mod 13: 12 \u2261 -1 mod 13.\n\nWe want mod 143. Solve:  \nx \u2261 0 mod 11  \nx \u2261 12 mod 13\n\nFrom x \u2261 0 mod 11 \u2192 x = 11k.  \n11k \u2261 12 mod 13.  \n11 \u2261 -2 mod 13, so -2k \u2261 12 \u2192 2k \u2261 1 mod 13 (multiply both sides by -1).  \nInverse of 2 mod 13 is 7 (since 2\u00d77=14\u22611). So k \u2261 7 mod 13.  \nSmallest k = 7 \u2192 x = 77.\n\nSo \\(58793812 \\equiv 77 \\bmod 143\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Combine mod 4 and mod 143**  \nWe have:  \nx \u2261 0 mod 4  \nx \u2261 77 mod 143.\n\nSince 143 \u2261 3 mod 4, check 77 mod 4 = 1. So 77 \u2261 1 mod 4, not 0. We need to add multiples of 143 until \u2261 0 mod 4.\n\n143 \u2261 3 mod 4. Adding 143 changes residue mod 4 by +3 (\u2261 -1 mod 4). We want to go from 1 to 0 mod 4, so need change of -1 mod 4. That means add 143 once:  \n77 + 143 = 220. Check: 220 mod 4 = 0. Good.\n\nSo x \u2261 220 mod (4\u00d7143=572).\n\nThus remainder = 220.\n\n---\n\n**Final ch