\n- \\( 572 \\times 80 = 45,760 \\)\n- Difference: \\( 49,412 - 45,760 = 3,652 \\)\n\n**Step 5: Continue with 3,652.**\n- \\( 572 \\times 6 = 3,432 \\)\n- Difference: \\( 3,652 - 3,432 = 220 \\)\n\nNow 220 is less than 572, so that\u2019s the remainder.\n\nLet\u2019s check quickly:\n\nWe had:\n- 100,000 + 2,000 + 700 + 80 + 6 = 102,786 as quotient, remainder 220.\n\nCheck: \\( 572 \\times 102,786 = ? \\)\n- \\( 572 \\times 100,000 = 57,200,000 \\)\n- \\( 572 \\times 2,786 = 1,593,? \\) Let's compute: \\( 572 \\times 2,786 = 572 \\times (2800 - 14) = 1,601,600 - 8,008 = 1,593,592 \\)\n- Sum: 57,200,000 + 1,593,592 = 58,793,592\n- Add remainder 220 \u2192 58,793,812 \u2713.\n\nSo remainder is **220**.\n\nAnswer: 220\nConfidence: 100%\n---\nFirst, note that 572 = 4 \u00d7 143 = 4 \u00d7 11 \u00d7 13.\n\nWe can find the remainder of 58793812 modulo 572 by computing modulo 4, 11, and 13, then using the Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n\n**Step 1: Mod 4**  \n58793812 mod 4: Last two digits are 12, and 12 mod 4 = 0.  \nSo remainder \u2261 0 mod 4.\n\n**Step 2: Mod 11**  \nCompute alternating sum of digits for 58793812:  \nDigits: 5,8,7,9,3,8,1,2  \n(Sum of digits in odd positions) \u2212 (sum in even positions):  \nOdd positions (1st,3rd,5th,7th): 5 + 7 + 3 + 1 = 16  \nEven positions: 8 + 9 + 8 + 2 = 27  \nDifference: 16 \u2212 27 = \u221211, and \u221211 \u2261 0 mod 11.  \nSo remainder \u2261 0 mod 11.\n\n**Step 3: Mod 13**  \nCompute 58793812 mod 13.  \n13 \u00d7 4,522,600 = 58,793,800. Subtract: 58,793,812 \u2212 58,793,800 = 12.  \nSo remainder \u2261 12 mod 13.\n\n**Step 4: Combine via CRT**  \nWe have:  \nN \u2261 0 mod 4  \nN \u2261 0 mod 11  \nN \u2261 12 mod 13\n\nFrom first two: N \u2261 0 mod 44.  \nSo N = 44k. Need 44k \u2261 12 mod 13.  \n44 mod 13 = 5, so 5k \u2261 12 mod 13.  \nInverse of 5 mod 13: 5\u00d78=40\u22611, so inverse is 8.  \nk \u2261 12\u00d78 = 96 \u2261 96\u221291=5 mod 13.  \nSo k = 5 + 13t.  \nN = 44(5 + 13t) = 220 + 572t.\n\nThus remainder mod 