\n\\(x \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5}\\)  \n\\(x \\equiv 3 \\pmod{11}\\)  \n\\(x \\equiv 11 \\pmod{13}\\)\n\nFirst combine mod 5 and 11:  \nFrom mod 5: \\(x = 5k\\).  \nFrom mod 11: \\(5k \\equiv 3 \\pmod{11}\\).  \nInverse of 5 mod 11 is 9 (since 5\u00d79=45\u22611).  \nSo \\(k \\equiv 3\u00d79 = 27 \u2261 5 \\pmod{11}\\).  \nThus \\(k = 11m + 5\\).  \nSo \\(x = 5(11m+5) = 55m + 25\\).\n\nNow mod 13: \\(55m + 25 \\equiv 11 \\pmod{13}\\).  \n55 mod 13 = 3 (since 13\u00d74=52, remainder 3).  \n25 mod 13 = 12.\n\nSo \\(3m + 12 \\equiv 11 \\pmod{13}\\).  \nThus \\(3m \\equiv -1 \\equiv 12 \\pmod{13}\\).  \nInverse of 3 mod 13 is 9 (3\u00d79=27\u22611).  \nSo \\(m \\equiv 12\u00d79 = 108 \u2261 4 \\pmod{13}\\) (since 13\u00d78=104, remainder 4).\n\nSo \\(m = 13t + 4\\).\n\nThen \\(x = 55(13t+4) + 25 = 715t + 220 + 25 = 715t + 245\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 6: Remainder.**\n\nThe remainder is \\(245\\).\n\n---\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{245}\n\\]\n\nAnswer: 245"
