    "raw": "Let's go step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder when \\( 2813410345 \\) is divided by \\( 508 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Break down the modulus**\n\n\\( 508 = 4 \\times 127 \\), and \\( \\gcd(4,127) = 1 \\).\n\nWe can find the remainder mod 4 and mod 127, then use the Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Mod 4**\n\nA number mod 4 depends on its last two digits.\n\nLast two digits of \\( 2813410345 \\) are 45.\n\n\\( 45 \\mod 4 = 1 \\).\n\nSo remainder mod 4 is 1.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Mod 127**\n\nWe can reduce \\( 2813410345 \\) mod 127.\n\nFirst, note \\( 10^3 = 1000 \\).  \n\\( 1000 \\mod 127 \\):  \n\\( 127 \\times 7 = 889 \\), remainder \\( 1000 - 889 = 111 \\).  \nSo \\( 1000 \\equiv 111 \\pmod{127} \\).\n\nBut maybe easier: break the number into chunks.\n\nLet\u2019s do it directly:\n\nWe can use the fact \\( 10^3 \\equiv 111 \\pmod{127} \\).\n\nWrite \\( 2813410345 \\) in base 1000 chunks:\n\n\\( 2,813,410,345 \\) \u2192 chunks: \\( 2 \\), \\( 813 \\), \\( 410 \\), \\( 345 \\).\n\nThat is:  \n\\( 2 \\times 1000^3 + 813 \\times 1000^2 + 410 \\times 1000 + 345 \\).\n\nNow mod 127:\n\n- \\( 1000 \\equiv 111 \\).\n- \\( 1000^2 \\equiv 111^2 = 12321 \\).  \n  \\( 12321 \\mod 127 \\):  \n  \\( 127 \\times 97 = 12319 \\), remainder 2.  \n  So \\( 1000^2 \\equiv 2 \\).\n- \\( 1000^3 \\equiv 111 \\times 2 = 222 \\).  \n  \\( 222 \\mod 127 = 95 \\).\n\nNow compute:\n\nTerm1: \\( 2 \\times 95 = 190 \\).  \n\\( 190 \\mod 127 = 63 \\).\n\nTerm2: \\( 813 \\times 2 = 1626 \\).  \n\\( 1626 \\mod 127 \\): \\( 127 \\times 12 = 1524 \\), remainder \\( 102 \\).\n\nTerm3: \\( 410 \\times 111 = 45510 \\).  \n\\( 45510 \\mod 127 \\):  \nFirst \\( 127 \\times 358 = 45466 \\), remainder \\( 44 \\).\n\nTerm4: \\( 345 \\mod 127 = 345 - 254 = 91 \\).\n\nSum: \\( 63 + 102 + 44 + 91 = 300 \\).  \n\\( 300 \\mod 127 = 300 - 254 = 46 \\).\n\nSo remainder mod 127 is 46.\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Solve CRT**\n\nWe have:\n\n\\( x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4} \\)  \n\\( x \\equiv 46 \\pmod{127} \\)\n\nFrom the second: \\( x = 46 + 127k \\).\n\nCheck mod 4: \\( 46 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4} \\), \\( 127 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\).\n\nSo \\( 2 + 3k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4} \\)  \n\\( 3k \\equiv -1 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\).\n\nMultiply inverse of 3 mod 4 (which is 3): \\( k \\equiv 9 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4} \\).\n\nSmallest \\( k = 1 \\): \\( x = 46 + 127 = 173 \\).\n\nCheck: \\( 173 \\mod 4 = 1 \\), \\( 173 \\mod 127 = 46 \\). Good.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5: Check if less than 508**\n\nYes, 173 < 508, so remainder is 173.\n\n---\n\n**Final answer:**\n\nAnswer: 173  \nConfidence: 95%"
