ossible values: 46, 173, 300, 427, \u2026  \nCheck mod 4:  \n46 mod 4 = 2 \u2192 no.  \n173 mod 4 = 1 \u2192 yes.\n\nSo \\(x = 173\\) is a solution mod \\(4 \\times 127 = 508\\).\n\nThus the remainder is 173.\n\n**Final check:** 173 < 508, matches both congruences.\n\nAnswer: 173  \nConfidence: 95%\n---\nLet's go step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder of \\( 2813410345 \\) when divided by \\( 508 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Check if easier modulus possible**\n\n\\( 508 = 4 \\times 127 \\).  \nWe could find remainder mod 4 and mod 127, then solve by Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Mod 4**\n\nA number mod 4 depends on last 2 digits.  \nLast 2 digits: 45.  \n\\( 45 \\mod 4 = 1 \\).\n\nSo remainder mod 4 is 1.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Mod 127**\n\nWe need \\( 2813410345 \\mod 127 \\).\n\nFirst, note \\( 10^3 = 1000 \\), and \\( 1000 \\mod 127 \\):  \n\\( 127 \\times 7 = 889\\), remainder \\(1000 - 889 = 111\\).  \nSo \\( 10^3 \\equiv 111 \\pmod{127} \\).  \nBut maybe better to just divide stepwise.\n\nAlternatively, note \\( 10^2 = 100 \\), \\( 10^3 \\equiv 1000 \\mod 127\\):\n\n\\(127 \\times 7 = 889\\), remainder 111.  \n\\(127 \\times 8 = 1016\\) too big. So yes 111.\n\nBut easier: Let's do long division mod 127.\n\n---\n\n**Step 4 \u2013 Break number into chunks**\n\nNumber: 2813410345.\n\nWe can process digits:\n\nStart with rem = 0.\n\n- Digit 2: rem = 2.\n- Next digit 8: rem = (2*10 + 8) = 28.\n- Next digit 1: rem = (28*10 + 1) = 281.  \n  \\(281 \\mod 127\\): \\(127 \\times 2 = 254\\), remainder 27.\n- Next digit 3: rem = (27*10 + 3) = 273.  \n  \\(273 - 127 \\times 2 = 273 - 254 = 19\\).\n- Next digit 4: rem = (19*10 + 4) = 194.  \n  \\(194 - 127 = 67\\).\n- Next digit 1: rem = (67*10 + 1) = 671.  \n  \\(127 \\times 5 = 635\\), remainder 36.\n- Next digit 0: rem = (36*10 + 0) = 360.  \n  \\(127 \\times 2 = 254\\), remainder 106.\n- Next digit 3: rem = (106*10 + 3) = 1063.  \n  \\(127 \\times 8 = 1016\\), remainder 47.\n- Next digit 4: rem = (47*10 + 4) = 474.  \n  \\(127 \\times 3 = 381\\), remainder 93.\n- Next digit 5: rem = (93*10 + 5) = 935.  \n  \\(127 \\times 7 = 889\\), remainder 46.\n\nSo remainder mod 127 is 46.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5 \u2013 Solve system**\n\nWe have:  \n\\( x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4} \\)  \n\\( x \\equiv 46 \\pmod{127} \\)\n\nFrom second: \\( x = 46 + 127k \\).  \nMod 4: \\( 46 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4} \\), \\(127 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\).\n\nSo \\( 2 + 3k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4} \\)  \n\\( 3k \\equiv -1 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\).  \nMultiply inverse of 3 mod 4 (which is 3): \\( k \\equiv 9 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4} \\).\n\nSo \\( k = 1 + 4t \\).\n\nThen \\( x = 46 + 127(1 + 4t) = 46 + 127 + 508t = 173 + 508t \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 6 \u2013 Remainder between 0 and 507**\n\nRemainder is 173.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 173  \nConfidence: 95%\n---\nLet\u2019s go step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder when \\( 2813410345 \\) is divided by \\( 508 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Simplify the problem using modular arithmetic.**\n\nWe can compute \\( 2813410345 \\mod 508 \\) directly, but it\u2019s easier to break it down.\n\nFirst, note \\( 508 = 4 \\times 127 \\).\n\nWe can find the remainder mod 4 and mod 127, then combine.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Mod 4.**\n\nA number mod 4 depends only on its last two digits: \\( 45 \\) mod 4 = 1.  \nSo \\( 2813410345 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4} \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Mod 127.**\n\nWe need \\( 2813410345 \\mod 127 \\).\n\nFirst, reduce 2813410345 step-by-step or in chunks.\n\nLet\u2019s use: \\( 10^3 = 1000 \\equiv 1000 - 7\\times127 = 1000 - 889 = 111 \\) mod 127.  \nBut maybe easier to just do long division.\n\nAlternatively, note \\( 127 \\times 22152837 = ? \\) \u2014 but that\u2019s messy.  \nLet\u2019s use modular reduction by grouping digits.\n\nNumber: 2,813,410,345.\n\nCheck mod 127:\n\nWe can do mod in parts:\n\nFirst, \\( 10^3 \\equiv 111 \\) (since 1000 - 7*127=111).  \n\\( 10^6 \\equiv 111^2 = 12321 \\) mod 127.  \n12321 \u00f7 127: 127\u00d797=12319, remainder 2