    "raw": "First, I\u2019ll compute \\( 9831076181 \\mod 215 \\).\n\nWe can break 215 into \\( 5 \\times 43 \\), but since they are coprime, we could also just do direct division.\n\nLet\u2019s do it step-by-step:\n\nFirst, note \\( 215 \\times 45,725,000 \\) would be too big, so let's find a closer multiple.\n\nWe can use modular reduction:\n\n- \\( 9831076181 \\mod 5 \\): last digit is 1, so remainder mod 5 is 1.\n- \\( 9831076181 \\mod 43 \\):\n\nLet\u2019s compute mod 43:\n\nFirst, \\( 43 \\times 228,629,000 = 9,831,047,000 \\) \u2014 too big? Let's check:  \nActually, \\( 43 \\times 228,629,000 = 9,831,047,000 \\), which is slightly less than 9,831,076,181. Difference: \\( 9,831,076,181 - 9,831,047,000 = 29,181 \\).\n\nNow reduce 29,181 mod 43:  \n43 \u00d7 678 = 29,154, remainder 27.  \nSo remainder mod 43 is 27.\n\nNow we need a number \\( x \\) such that:  \n\\( x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\)  \n\\( x \\equiv 27 \\pmod{43} \\)\n\nLet \\( x = 27 + 43k \\).  \nMod 5: \\( 27 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\), \\( 43 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\).  \nSo \\( 2 + 3k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\) \u2192 \\( 3k \\equiv -1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\).  \nInverse of 3 mod 5 is 2 (since 3\u00d72=6\u22611), so \\( k \\equiv 4\u00d72 = 8 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\).\n\nSmallest k=3: \\( x = 27 + 43\u00d73 = 27 + 129 = 156 \\).\n\nCheck: 156 mod 5 = 1, 156 mod 43 = 27. Good.\n\nSo remainder is 156.\n\nAnswer: 156  \nConfidence: 95%"
