    "raw": "Let's go step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder when \\( 2132637511 \\) is divided by \\( 546 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Factor the divisor**  \n\\( 546 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7 \\times 13 \\).\n\nWe can find the remainder modulo each factor and then use the Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Modulo 2**  \nThe number is odd (ends in 1), so:\n\n\\[\n2132637511 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Modulo 3**  \nSum of digits: \\(2+1+3+2+6+3+7+5+1+1 = 31\\).  \n\\(31 \\bmod 3 = 1\\), so:\n\n\\[\n2132637511 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Modulo 7**  \nLet\u2019s compute \\(2132637511 \\bmod 7\\).\n\nWe can do this stepwise:\n\n- \\(10^0 \\equiv 1\\)  \n- \\(10^1 \\equiv 3\\)  \n- \\(10^2 \\equiv 2\\)  \n- \\(10^3 \\equiv 6\\)  \n- \\(10^4 \\equiv 4\\)  \n- \\(10^5 \\equiv 5\\)  \n- \\(10^6 \\equiv 1\\) (cycle length 6)\n\nNumber: 2,132,637,511 \u2192 digits from right to left: 1,1,5,7,3,6,2,3,1,2.\n\nPowers of 10 mod 7 cycle: 1, 3, 2, 6, 4, 5, repeat.\n\nPositions (starting at 0 for units):\n\n- pos0: 1 \u00d7 1 = 1  \n- pos1: 1 \u00d7 3 = 3  \n- pos2: 5 \u00d7 2 = 10 \u2261 3  \n- pos3: 7 \u00d7 6 = 42 \u2261 0  \n- pos4: 3 \u00d7 4 = 12 \u2261 5  \n- pos5: 6 \u00d7 5 = 30 \u2261 2  \n- pos6: 2 \u00d7 1 = 2  \n- pos7: 3 \u00d7 3 = 9 \u2261 2  \n- pos8: 1 \u00d7 2 = 2  \n- pos9: 2 \u00d7 6 = 12 \u2261 5  \n\nSum: \\(1+3+3+0+5+2+2+2+2+5 = 25\\).  \n\\(25 \\bmod 7 = 4\\).\n\nSo remainder mod 7 is 4.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5: Modulo 13**  \nPowers of 10 mod 13:  \n10^0=1, 10^1=10, 10^2=9, 10^3=12, 10^4=3, 10^5=4, 10^6=1 (cycle length 6).\n\nDigits from right: 1,1,5,7,3,6,2,3,1,2.\n\n- pos0: 1\u00d71=1  \n- pos1: 1\u00d710=10  \n- pos2: 5\u00d79=45\u22616  \n- pos3: 7\u00d712=84\u22616  \n- pos4: 3\u00d73=9  \n- pos5: 6\u00d74=24\u226111  \n- pos6: 2\u00d71=2  \n- pos7: 3\u00d710=30\u22614  \n- pos8: 1\u00d79=9  \n- pos9: 2\u00d712=24\u226111  \n\nSum: 1+10=11, +6=17, +6=23, +9=32, +11=43, +2=45, +4=49, +9=58, +11=69.  \n69 mod 13: 13\u00d75=65, remainder 4.\n\nSo remainder mod 13 is 4.\n\n---\n\n**Step 6: Combine using CRT**  \nWe have:\n\n- mod 2: 1  \n- mod 3: 1  \n- mod 7: 4  \n- mod 13: 4  \n\nFirst combine mod 7 and mod 13: both \u22614, so mod 91 it's 4 (since 4 mod 7=4, 4 mod 13=4).\n\nNow combine mod 2 and mod 3: both \u22611, so mod 6 it's 1.\n\nWe need x \u2261 4 (mod 91) and x \u2261 1 (mod 6).\n\nCheck numbers \u22614 mod 91: 4, 95, 186, 277, 368, 459, 550, ...\n\nCheck mod 6:  \n4 mod 6=4, 95 mod 6=5, 186 mod 6=0, 277 mod 6=1 \u2192 matches.\n\nSo x \u2261 277 mod lcm(91,6)=546.\n\n---\n\n**Step 7: Conclusion**  \nRemainder is 277.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 277  \nConfidence: 95%"
