    "raw": "Let's compute \\( 99721481 \\mod 875 \\).\n\nFirst, note that \\( 875 = 5^3 \\times 7 = 125 \\times 7 \\).\n\nWe can find the remainder by direct division or modular arithmetic.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Break into mod 125 and mod 7, then use Chinese Remainder Theorem.**\n\n**Mod 125:**\n- \\( 1000 \\equiv 0 \\mod 125\\), so we can take the last 3 digits for mod 125? Actually, \\(1000\\) is divisible by 125, so \\(99721481 \\mod 125\\) is determined by last 3 digits: \\(481\\).\n- \\(481 \\mod 125\\): \\(125 \\times 3 = 375\\), remainder \\(481 - 375 = 106\\).\nSo remainder mod 125 is 106.\n\n**Mod 7:**\n- Compute \\(99721481 \\mod 7\\).\n- First, \\(10^6 \\equiv 1 \\mod 7\\) (since 10 \u2261 3, 3^6 \u2261 1 mod 7). But easier: break number into chunks or do stepwise.\n- Let's do stepwise:\n  - Start with 0.\n  - 9 mod 7 = 2.\n  - (2*10 + 9) = 29 mod 7 = 1.\n  - (1*10 + 7) = 17 mod 7 = 3.\n  - (3*10 + 2) = 32 mod 7 = 4.\n  - (4*10 + 1) = 41 mod 7 = 6.\n  - (6*10 + 4) = 64 mod 7 = 1.\n  - (1*10 + 8) = 18 mod 7 = 4.\n  - (4*10 + 1) = 41 mod 7 = 6.\n\nSo remainder mod 7 is 6.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Solve CRT:**\nWe need \\(x \\equiv 106 \\pmod{125}\\) and \\(x \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\nFrom \\(x \\equiv 106 \\pmod{125}\\), \\(x = 106 + 125k\\).\n\nMod 7: \\(106 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\) (since 105 is multiple of 7, remainder 1).  \n\\(125 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}\\) (since 119 is multiple of 7, remainder 6).\n\nSo \\(1 + 6k \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}\\)  \n\\(\\Rightarrow 6k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\nMultiply inverse of 6 mod 7: 6 \u2261 -1, inverse is -1 \u2261 6.  \nSo \\(k \\equiv 5 \\times 6 = 30 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\nSmallest k = 2.\n\nThen \\(x = 106 + 125\\times 2 = 106 + 250 = 356\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Check:**\n356 mod 125 = 106 \u2705  \n356 mod 7: 7\u00d750=350, remainder 6 \u2705\n\nSo remainder is 356.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 356  \nConfidence: 95%"
