    "raw": "Let\u2019s go through the problem step-by-step.\n\nWe want the remainder when \\( 99721481 \\) is divided by \\( 875 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Factor or simplify the modulus**  \n\\( 875 = 5^3 \\times 7 = 125 \\times 7 \\).  \nWe can find the remainder mod 125 and mod 7, then combine using the Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Mod 125**  \nSince \\( 1000 \\) is a multiple of 125, we can look at the last three digits of the number:  \nLast three digits of \\( 99721481 \\) are \\( 481 \\).\n\n481 mod 125:  \n\\( 125 \\times 3 = 375 \\)  \n\\( 481 - 375 = 106 \\).\n\nSo remainder mod 125 = 106.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Mod 7**  \nWe can reduce 99721481 mod 7.  \nFirst, note \\( 10^6 \\equiv 1 \\mod 7 \\) (since 10 \u2261 3, and 3^6 \u2261 1 mod 7 by Fermat\u2019s little theorem). But easier: process digits or larger chunks.\n\nLet\u2019s do it with chunks:  \nBreak 99721481 into 99,721,481? Actually, maybe use mod directly:  \n\n10 mod 7 = 3.  \nLet\u2019s compute stepwise:\n\nStart with 0:  \nDigit 9: (0\u00d710 + 9) = 9 mod 7 = 2  \nNext digit 9: (2\u00d710 + 9) = 29 mod 7 = 1  \nNext digit 7: (1\u00d710 + 7) = 17 mod 7 = 3  \nNext digit 2: (3\u00d710 + 2) = 32 mod 7 = 4  \nNext digit 1: (4\u00d710 + 1) = 41 mod 7 = 6  \nNext digit 4: (6\u00d710 + 4) = 64 mod 7 = 1  \nNext digit 8: (1\u00d710 + 8) = 18 mod 7 = 4  \nNext digit 1: (4\u00d710 + 1) = 41 mod 7 = 6\n\nSo remainder mod 7 = 6.\n\n---\n\n**Step 4 \u2013 Solve system**  \nWe have:  \n\\( x \\equiv 106 \\pmod{125} \\)  \n\\( x \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\)\n\nFrom first equation: \\( x = 106 + 125k \\).  \nMod 7: 106 mod 7 = (105+1) \u2192 1, and 125 mod 7 = 6.\n\nSo \\( 1 + 6k \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\)  \n\\( 6k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\)  \nMultiply inverse of 6 mod 7 (which is 6, since 6\u00d76=36\u22611):  \n\\( k \\equiv 5\u00d76 = 30 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\).\n\nSo k = 2 + 7t. Smallest positive k = 2.\n\nThen x = 106 + 125\u00d72 = 106 + 250 = 356.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5 \u2013 Check**  \n356 mod 125 = 106 \u2705  \n356 mod 7: 7\u00d750=350, remainder 6 \u2705\n\nSince 356 < 875, that\u2019s the final remainder.\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nAnswer: 356  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nFirst, I\u2019ll compute \\( 99721481 \\mod 875 \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Break down the modulus**  \n\\( 875 = 7 \\times 125 \\), but since 125 and 7 are coprime, maybe easier to just divide directly.\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Direct computation**  \nWe want \\( 99721481 \\div 875 \\).\n\nFirst, note \\( 875 \\times 100000 = 87500000 \\).  \nDifference: \\( 99721481 - 87500000 = 12221481 \\).\n\nNow \\( 875 \\times 10000 = 8750000 \\).  \nSubtract from 12221481: \\( 12221481 - 8750000 = 3471481 \\).\n\n\\( 875 \\times 3000 = 2625000 \\).  \nSubtract: \\( 3471481 - 2625000 = 846481 \\).\n\n\\( 875 \\times 900 = 787500 \\).  \nSubtract: \\( 846481 - 787500 = 58981 \\).\n\n\\( 875 \\times 60 = 52500 \\).  \nSubtract: \\( 58981 - 52500 = 6481 \\).\n\n\\( 875 \\times 7 = 6125 \\).  \nSubtract: \\( 6481 - 6125 = 356 \\).\n\n356 < 875, so remainder is 356.\n\n---\n\nLet\u2019s verify quickly modulo smaller factors:  \nMod 7: \\( 99721481 \\mod 7 \\) \u2014 99721481 / 7: 7\u00d714245926 = 99721482, which is 1 too big, so remainder is -1 \u2261 6 mod 7.  \n356 mod 7 = 356 - 7\u00d750=356-350=6 \u2714.\n\nMod 125: last 3 digits 481 mod 125 = 481 - 375 = 106.  \n356 mod 125 = 356 - 250 = 106 \u2714.\n\nSo remainder matches both.\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nAnswer: 356  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nLet's compute \\( 99721481 \\mod 875 \\).\n\nFirst, note that:\n\\[\n875 = 7 \\times 125\n\\]\n\nWe can find the remainder by using modular arithmetic.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Modulo 125\n125 divides 1000, so let's look at last 3 digits.\n\nLast 3 digits of 99721481 are 481.\n\n\\[\n481 \\mod 125\n\\]\n125 \u00d7 3 = 375, remainder = 481 \u2212 375 = 106.\n\nSo:\n\\[\n99721481 \\equiv 106 \\pmod{125}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Modulo 7\nWe can take the whole number modulo 7.\n\nFirst, \\(10^6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\) (Fermat's little theorem: \\(10^6 \\equiv 1\\) mod 7). But let's just do direct modular reduction.\n\nWe can do it stepwise:\n\nStart with 0:\n\n- 9 mod 7 = 2  \n- 29 mod 7 = 1 (since 2\u00d710+9=29, 29 mod 7 = 1)  \nActually better to do digit-by-digit:\n\nNumber: 9 9 7 2 1 4 8 1\n\nProcess:\n\n1. Start r = 0  \n2. r = (0\u00d710 + 9) mod 7 = 2  \n3. r = (2\u00d710 + 9) = 29 mod 7 = 1  \n4. r = (1\u00d710 + 7) = 17 mod 7 = 3  \n5. r = (3\u00d710 + 2) = 32 mod 7 = 4  \n6. r = (4\u00d710 + 1) = 41 mod 7 = 6  \n7. r = (6\u00d710 + 4) = 64 mod 7 = 1  \n8. r = (1\u00d710 + 8) = 18 mod 7 = 4  \n9. r = (4\u00d710 + 1) = 41 mod 7 = 6\n\nSo remainder mod 7 is 6.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Combine via Chinese Remainder Theorem\nWe have:\n\\[\nx \\equiv 106 \\pmod{125}\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\nLet \\( x = 106 + 125k \\).  \nWe need \\( 106 + 125k \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\).\n\n106 mod 7: 7\u00d715=105, remainder 1.  \n125 mod 7: 7\u00d717=119, remainder 6 (or -1).\n\nSo:\n\\[\n1 + 6k \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\n6k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nMultiply inverse of 6 mod 7: 6 inverse is 6 (since 6\u00d76=36\u22611).  \nSo \\( k \\equiv 5\u00d76 = 30 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\).\n\nSo smallest k = 2.\n\nThen:\n\\[\nx = 106 + 125\u00d72 = 106 + 250 = 356\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Check\n356 mod 125 = 106 \u2713  \n356 mod 7: 7\u00d750=350, remainder 6 \u2713\n\n---\n\n**Final remainder**: \\( \\mathbf{356} \\)\n\nAnswer: 356  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nFirst, I\u2019ll calculate \\( 99721481 \\mod 875 \\).\n\nWe can proceed step-by-step.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Break down the modulus**\n\n\\( 875 = 125 \\times 7 \\), and \\(125 = 5^3\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Mod 125 first**\n\nSince 1000 is divisible by 125, we can take the last 3 digits of 99721481.  \nThe last 3 digits are 481.\n\n\\( 481 \\mod 125 \\):  \n125 \u00d7 3 = 375, remainder = 481 \u2212 375 = 106.\n\nSo \\( 99721481 \\equiv 106 \\pmod{125} \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Mod 7 next**\n\nWe can use the fact that \\(1000 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{7}\\) (since 1001 is divisible by 7).\n\nWrite 99,721,481 in groups of 3 from the right:  \n99 | 721 | 481.\n\nAlternating sum method for mod 7:\n\n- Group1: 481 (units group) \u2192 +481  \n- Group2: 721 \u2192 \u2212721  \n- Group3: 99 \u2192 +99\n\nSum = 481 \u2212 721 + 99 = (481 + 99) \u2212 721 = 580 \u2212 721 = \u2212141.\n\nNow \\( -141 \\mod 7 \\):  \n141 = 7 \u00d7 20 + 1, so \u2212141 \u2261 \u22121 \u2261 6 mod 7.\n\nSo \\( 99721481 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 4 \u2013 Combine via CRT**\n\nWe have:\n\n\\( x \\equiv 106 \\pmod{125} \\)  \n\\( x \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\)\n\nLet \\( x = 106 + 125k \\). We want \\( 106 + 125k \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\).\n\n106 mod 7: 7 \u00d7 15 = 105, remainder 1.  \n125 mod 7: 7 \u00d7 17 = 119, remainder 6 (or \u22121).\n\nSo: \\( 1 + 6k \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\).  \nThus \\( 6k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\).\n\n6 \u2261 \u22121 mod 7, so \u2212k \u2261 5 \u2192 k \u2261 \u22125 \u2261 2 mod 7.\n\nSmallest k = 2.\n\nThen x = 106 + 125\u00d72 = 106 + 250 = 356.\n\n356 < 875, so it\u2019s the final remainder.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5 \u2013 Check**\n\n356 mod 125 = 106 \u2714  \n356 mod 7: 7\u00d750 = 350, remainder 6 \u2714\n\nSo remainder is indeed 356.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 356  \nConfidence: 95%\n---\nLet\u2019s compute step-by-step.\n\nWe want \\( 99721481 \\mod 875 \\).\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Simplify using modular arithmetic.**\n\nFirst, note \\( 875 = 7 \\times 125 \\).  \nBut we can just do direct division.\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 Try breaking the number.**  \nActually, easier:  \nWe know \\( 1000 \\equiv 125 \\pmod{875} \\), but maybe just do division.\n\n875 \u00d7 100,000 = 87,500,000 which is too big, but 875 \u00d7 100,000 = 87,500,000 (wait, that's off \u2014 recheck: 875 \u00d7 100,000 = 87,500,000? No: 875\u00d7100,000 = 87,500,000? Let's check: 875\u00d7100,000 = 87,500,000? Actually 875 \u00d7 100,000 = 87,500,000 \u2014 yes correct, but our number is ~99 million). So 875 \u00d7 100,000 = 87,500,000.  \nDifference: 99,721,481 \u2212 87,500,000 = 12,221,481.\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 Continue dividing 12,221,481 by 875.**\n\n875 \u00d7 10,000 = 8,750,000. Subtract: 12,221,481 \u2212 8,750,000 = 3,471,481.\n\n875 \u00d7 3,000 = 2,625,000. Subtract: 3,471,481 \u2212 2,625,000 = 846,481.\n\n875 \u00d7 900 = 787,500. Subtract: 846,481 \u2212 787,500 = 58,981.\n\n875 \u00d7 60 = 52,500. Subtract: 58,981 \u2212 52,500 = 6,481.\n\n875 \u00d7 7 = 6,125. Subtract: 6,481 \u2212 6,125 = 356.\n\n356 < 875, so remainder = 356.\n\n**Step 4 \u2013 Double-check quickly by modulo properties.**\n\nWe can check mod 7 and mod 125:\n\n- Mod 7: 99721481.  \n10^6 \u2261 1 mod 7? Actually 10 \u2261 3, 10^6 \u2261 1 mod 7, but easier: 99,721,481 mod 7.  \n7\u00d714,245,925 = 99,721,475, remainder 6. So \u2261 6 mod 7.\n\nOur remainder 356 mod 7: 7\u00d750=350, remainder 6 \u2014 matches.\n\n- Mod 125: 99721481 mod 125. Last 3 digits: 481 mod 125 = 481 \u2212 375 = 106.  \n356 mod 125 = 356 \u2212 250 = 106 \u2014 matches.\n\nThus remainder = 356 is correct.\n\n**Final answer:**\n\nAnswer: 356\nConfidence: 100%"
