    "raw": "Let's compute the remainder when 6714205543 is divided by 658.\n\nFirst, note that:\n- 658 = 2 \u00d7 329, and 329 = 7 \u00d7 47, so 658 = 2 \u00d7 7 \u00d7 47.\n\nWe can find 6714205543 mod 658 step-by-step.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Mod 2**\n6714205543 is odd, so remainder mod 2 is 1.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Mod 7**\nFirst, compute 6714205543 mod 7.\n\nWe can use 10 mod 7 = 3, but let's do direct division:\n\n7 \u00d7 959172220 = 6714205540, remainder 3.  \nSo mod 7 = 3.\n\nCheck: 959172220 \u00d7 7 = 6714205540, yes remainder 3.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Mod 47**\nCompute 6714205543 mod 47.\n\nFirst, 47 \u00d7 142855437 = 6714205539, remainder 4.\n\nCheck: 142855437 \u00d7 47 = 142855437 \u00d7 (50 \u2212 3) = 7142771850 \u2212 428566311 = 6714205539, yes remainder 4.\n\nSo mod 47 = 4.\n\n---\n\nWe have:\n- mod 2 \u2192 1\n- mod 7 \u2192 3\n- mod 47 \u2192 4\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Combine mod 7 and mod 47 (mod 329)**\nLet x \u2261 3 (mod 7), x \u2261 4 (mod 47).\n\nFrom x \u2261 3 mod 7, x = 3 + 7k.\n\nPlug into mod 47: 3 + 7k \u2261 4 mod 47 \u2192 7k \u2261 1 mod 47.\n\nFind inverse of 7 mod 47: 7 \u00d7 27 = 189 \u2261 1 mod 47 (since 47\u00d74=188, remainder 1). Yes, inverse is 27.\n\nSo k \u2261 27 \u00d7 1 = 27 mod 47.\n\nThus x = 3 + 7\u00d727 = 3 + 189 = 192 mod 329.\n\nSo x \u2261 192 mod 329.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5: Combine with mod 2**\nWe need x \u2261 192 mod 329 and x \u2261 1 mod 2.\n\nSince 329 is odd, 192 mod 329 is even (192 even). But we need odd (mod 2 = 1). So add 329 (odd) once to make it odd: 192 + 329 = 521 (odd, good).\n\nCheck: 521 mod 329 = 192, yes, and 521 mod 2 = 1.\n\nSo remainder mod 658 is 521.\n\n---\n\n**Step 6: Verify**\n658 \u00d7 10203929 = ?\n\nLet\u2019s check: 658 \u00d7 10,203,929 = 658 \u00d7 10,000,000 = 6,580,000,000; plus 658 \u00d7 203,929 = 658\u00d7200,000=131,600,000; plus 658\u00d73,929=2,585,282; sum: 6,580,000,000 + 131,600,000 = 6,711,600,000; + 2,585,282 = 6,714,185,282. Then add remainder 521 \u2192 6,714,185,803. But original number is 6,714,205,543 \u2014 wait, that's different.\n\nLet me recheck multiplication:\n\nBetter: 6714205543 \u00f7 658.\n\n658 \u00d7 10,203,929 = ?\n\n658 \u00d7 10,000,000 = 6,580,000,000  \n658 \u00d7 203,929:  \n658 \u00d7 200,000 = 131,600,000  \n658 \u00d7 3,929 = 658\u00d73,900=2,566,200; plus 658\u00d729=19,082; total 2,585,282.  \nSum: 131,600,000 + 2,585,282 = 134,185,282.  \nAdd to 6,580,000,000 \u2192 6,714,185,282.  \n\nDifference to original: 6,714,205,543 \u2212 6,714,185,282 = 20,261. This is larger than 658, so quotient too small. Let's add more: 20,261 \u00f7 658 \u2248 30.79, so add 30 to quotient.\n\n658 \u00d7 30 = 19,740. Subtract: 20,261 \u2212 19,740 = 521 remainder.\n\nSo quotient = 10,203,929 + 30 = 10,203,959, remainder 521.\n\nCheck: 658 \u00d7 10,203,959 = 6,714,185,282 + 19,740 = 6,714,205,022; plus remainder 521 = 6,714,205,543. Correct.\n\n---\n\nSo the remainder is indeed 521.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 521  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nFirst, I\u2019ll divide 6714205543 by 658 step-by-step.\n\nWe can use modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, note:\n658 = 2 \u00d7 7 \u00d7 47. But maybe simpler to just do direct division.\n\nLet\u2019s estimate:\n\n658 \u00d7 10,000,000 = 6,580,000,000 \u2014 too big? Wait, 6714205543 is ~6.7 billion, so 10 million multiplier is okay.\n\n6,714,205,543 \u00f7 658.\n\nCheck 658 \u00d7 10,000,000 = 6,580,000,000 \u2014 difference is 134,205,543.\n\nNow, 658 \u00d7 200,000 = 131,600,000.  \nSubtract: 134,205,543 \u2212 131,600,000 = 2,605,543.\n\nNext, 658 \u00d7 3,000 = 1,974,000.  \nSubtract: 2,605,543 \u2212 1,974,000 = 631,543.\n\nNext, 658 \u00d7 900 = 592,200.  \nSubtract: 631,543 \u2212 592,200 = 39,343.\n\nNext, 658 \u00d7 50 = 32,900.  \nSubtract: 39,343 \u2212 32,900 = 6,443.\n\nNext, 658 \u00d7 9 = 5,922.  \nSubtract: 6,443 \u2212 5,922 = 521.\n\n521 is less than 658, so remainder is 521.\n\nLet\u2019s sum multipliers: 10,000,000 + 200,000 + 3,000 + 900 + 50 + 9 = 10,203,959.  \nCheck: 658 \u00d7 10,203,959 = ?\n\n658 \u00d7 10,000,000 = 6,580,000,000  \n658 \u00d7 203,959:\n\n658 \u00d7 200,000 = 131,600,000  \n658 \u00d7 3,959 = 658 \u00d7 4,000 \u2212 658 \u00d7 41 = 2,632,000 \u2212 26,978 = 2,605,022.\n\nSo 131,600,000 + 2,605,022 = 134,205,022.\n\nAdd to 6,580,000,000 = 6,714,205,022.  \nOriginal number: 6,714,205,543. Difference = 521. Confirmed.\n\nAnswer: 521  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nLet\u2019s go step-by-step.\n\nWe want \\( 6714205543 \\mod 658 \\).\n\nFirst, note that 658 factors as \\( 2 \\times 329 \\) and 329 = 7 \u00d7 47.  \nSo \\( 658 = 2 \\times 7 \\times 47 \\).\n\nWe can do the division directly.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 approximate division**  \n658 \u00d7 10,000,000 = 6,580,000,000, which is slightly less than 6,714,205,543.\n\nDifference:  \n6,714,205,543 \u2212 6,580,000,000 = 134,205,543.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 divide the remainder further**  \n658 \u00d7 200,000 = 131,600,000.\n\n134,205,543 \u2212 131,600,000 = 2,605,543.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 continue**  \n658 \u00d7 3,000 = 1,974,000.\n\n2,605,543 \u2212 1,974,000 = 631,543.\n\n---\n\n**Step 4 \u2013 continue**  \n658 \u00d7 900 = 592,200.\n\n631,543 \u2212 592,200 = 39,343.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5 \u2013 continue**  \n658 \u00d7 50 = 32,900.\n\n39,343 \u2212 32,900 = 6,443.\n\n---\n\n**Step 6 \u2013 continue**  \n658 \u00d7 9 = 5,922.\n\n6,443 \u2212 5,922 = 521.\n\n---\n\nSince 521 < 658, this is the remainder.\n\n---\n\n**Double-check via modular arithmetic:**  \nLet's check mod 2: number ends with 3 \u2192 odd \u2192 remainder mod 2 = 1. 521 is odd \u2192 matches.  \nMod 7: 6714205543 mod 7.  \nLet\u2019s check: 6,714,205,543 \u00f7 7 \u2192 7 \u00d7 959,172,220 = 6,714,205,540, remainder 3. So mod 7 = 3.  \nNow 521 mod 7: 7 \u00d7 74 = 518, remainder 3 \u2192 matches.  \nMod 47: 6714205543 mod 47.  \n47 \u00d7 142,855,437 = 6,714,205,539, remainder 4. So mod 47 = 4.  \n521 mod 47: 47 \u00d7 11 = 517, remainder 4 \u2192 matches.\n\nSo remainder is consistent.\n\n**Final remainder:** 521.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 521  \nConfidence: 100%\n---\nLet's compute step by step.\n\nWe want \\( 6714205543 \\mod 658 \\).\n\nFirst, note \\( 658 = 2 \\times 329 \\), and \\(329 = 7 \\times 47\\), so \\(658 = 2 \\times 7 \\times 47\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Mod 2**  \n6714205543 is odd \u2192 remainder 1 mod 2.\n\n**Step 2: Mod 7**  \nLet's find mod 7.\n\nCompute 6714205543 mod 7:  \nFirst, 6,714,205,543.  \n7 \u00d7 959,172,220 = 6,714,205,540 \u2192 remainder 3.  \nSo mod 7 = 3.\n\n**Step 3: Mod 47**  \nWe can do this by long division or using mod properties.  \nFirst, note that 47 \u00d7 142,855,437 = 6,714,205,539 \u2192 remainder 4.  \nLet's check: 142,855,437 \u00d7 47:  \n142,855,437 \u00d7 40 = 5,714,217,480  \n142,855,437 \u00d7 7 = 999,988,059  \nSum = 6,714,205,539 \u2192 yes, remainder 4.\n\nSo mod 47 = 4.\n\n---\n\nNow we have:\n- \\( x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2} \\)\n- \\( x \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\)\n- \\( x \\equiv 4 \\pmod{47} \\)\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Combine mod 7 and 47 first (since coprime)**  \nWe need x \u2261 3 mod 7, x \u2261 4 mod 47.\n\nLet x = 4 + 47k.  \n4 + 47k \u2261 3 mod 7  \n47 \u2261 5 mod 7, so:  \n4 + 5k \u2261 3 mod 7 \u2192 5k \u2261 -1 \u2261 6 mod 7.  \nMultiply inverse of 5 mod 7 (which is 3):  \nk \u2261 18 \u2261 4 mod 7.\n\nSo k = 4 + 7t.  \nx = 4 + 47(4 + 7t) = 4 + 188 + 329t = 192 + 329t.\n\nSo x \u2261 192 mod 329.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5: Incorporate mod 2 condition**  \nWe have x \u2261 192 mod 329, and x \u2261 1 mod 2.\n\n329 \u2261 1 mod 2, 192 \u2261 0 mod 2.  \nSo x = 192 + 329t \u2261 0 + 1\u00d7t \u2261 t mod 2.  \nWe need t \u2261 1 mod 2.\n\nSo t = 1 + 2s.  \nx = 192 + 329(1 + 2s) = 192 + 329 + 658s = 521 + 658s.\n\nThus x \u2261 521 mod 658.\n\n---\n\n**Step 6: Conclusion**  \nThe remainder is 521.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 521  \nConfidence: 95%\n---\nLet's divide 6,714,205,543 by 658.\n\nFirst, estimate:  \n658 \u00d7 10,000,000 = 6,580,000,000  \nDifference: 6,714,205,543 \u2212 6,580,000,000 = 134,205,543.\n\n658 \u00d7 2