    "raw": "Let's compute \\( 74309920 \\mod 165 \\).\n\nFirst, note \\( 165 = 3 \\times 5 \\times 11 \\).\n\nWe can find the remainder modulo each factor and then use the Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n\n---\n\n**Modulo 3:**  \nSum of digits of 74309920: \\( 7+4+3+0+9+9+2+0 = 34 \\).  \n34 mod 3 = 1.  \nSo remainder mod 3 is 1.\n\n---\n\n**Modulo 5:**  \nLast digit is 0, so remainder mod 5 is 0.\n\n---\n\n**Modulo 11:**  \nAlternating sum: (from right to left)  \nDigits: 7 4 3 0 9 9 2 0  \nPositions from right (starting at 1):  \npos1: 0, pos2: 2, pos3: 9, pos4: 9, pos5: 0, pos6: 3, pos7: 4, pos8: 7.  \nSum of odd positions: pos1+pos3+pos5+pos7 = 0 + 9 + 0 + 4 = 13.  \nSum of even positions: pos2+pos4+pos6+pos8 = 2 + 9 + 3 + 7 = 21.  \nDifference: 13 - 21 = -8.  \n-8 mod 11 = 3.  \nSo remainder mod 11 is 3.\n\n---\n\nWe need \\( x \\) such that:  \n\\( x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\)  \n\\( x \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\)  \n\\( x \\equiv 3 \\pmod{11} \\)\n\nFrom mod 5: \\( x = 5k \\).  \nFrom mod 3: \\( 5k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\), \\( 5 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3} \\), so \\( 2k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\).  \nMultiply inverse of 2 mod 3 (which is 2): \\( k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3} \\).  \nSo \\( k = 3m + 2 \\), hence \\( x = 5(3m+2) = 15m + 10 \\).\n\nNow mod 11: \\( 15m + 10 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{11} \\).  \n15 \u2261 4 mod 11, so \\( 4m + 10 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{11} \\).  \nThus \\( 4m \\equiv -7 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{11} \\).  \nMultiply inverse of 4 mod 11 (which is 3): \\( m \\equiv 12 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11} \\).  \nSo \\( m = 11t + 1 \\).\n\nThen \\( x = 15(11t+1) + 10 = 165t + 15 + 10 = 165t + 25 \\).\n\nSo remainder is 25.\n\n---\n\nAnswer: 25  \nConfidence: 100%"
